WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.843
Hi, I'm Toffee!

00:00:02.843 --> 00:00:07.373
Cinference runs a twenty-seven billion parameter model at four hundred fifty tokens

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.260
a second, for one user.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.015
Here's how.

00:00:11.808 --> 00:00:14.834
Four hundred fifty isn't a faster forward pass.

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:18.239
It's one pass checking ten drafted tokens at once.

00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:21.265
With speculation off, the same card does seventy-one.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.416
Each round, a draft head guesses ten tokens, and the full model

00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:27.881
checks them in one pass.

00:00:28.301 --> 00:00:31.814
K is the window: ten, up from five in the upstream engine.

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:36.240
Acceptance is how often the drafts are kept: ninety-three percent at eight

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.576
K, ninety-eight at two hundred sixty K.

00:00:38.996 --> 00:00:42.258
So each round yields ten point three one tokens, rising to

00:00:42.258 --> 00:00:43.444
ten point eight five.

00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:49.237
The repository installs all this from one menu: build the engine, download

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:50.929
the model, start the server.

00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:55.398
Its header names the setup every number here depends on: one card,

00:00:55.398 --> 00:00:57.759
the ten-token draft, and the compressed cache.

00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:02.075
The sixty-four layers come in blocks of four.

00:01:02.075 --> 00:01:05.846
Three are gated DeltaNet, with a fixed-size state that never grows.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.831
Only every fourth layer is full attention, sixteen in all, and only

00:01:09.831 --> 00:01:12.505
those keep a cache that grows with the sequence.

00:01:12.925 --> 00:01:16.943
That cache is compressed: rotated by a fixed Hadamard matrix, then keys

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:19.286
stored in eight bits, values in four.

00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:24.618
So the speed is a statement about the workload.

00:01:24.618 --> 00:01:27.641
Recall copies text and accepts nearly every draft.

00:01:27.641 --> 00:01:31.419
Story writing accepts thirty-seven percent, and lands at one twenty-six.

00:01:33.142 --> 00:01:36.615
A speculative throughput is a statement about the workload.

00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:41.246
Long context holds because 48 of 64 layers keep no growing cache.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.908
Draft ten, check once, and keep the cache small by design.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.351
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.351 --> 00:01:49.951
I'm Toffee.

00:01:49.951 --> 00:01:50.251
Bye!
